e want to minimize  suffering and in part what that means  is that those of us um in the industrial  world  countries like the united states that  have this legacy of  two centuries of access to cheap uh  dirty energy  we have an obligation to help other  countries um  like india uh the rest of uh the  developing world to  first of all leap frog past the fossil  fuel stage  so they don't have to go through it we  need to provide them resources  renewable energy access uh to do that  and we need to help them out provide  them resources  to give them greater resilience and ceci  i could see you nodding there so how  should those resources be spent what are  the best examples  of adaptation a really good example  of climate adaptation i think is a case  of um let's say in the uk um the uh high  speed  uh two rail net rail network which is  supposed to run between london  and birmingham and leeds and it's  supposed to be the biggest you know  second biggest  um rail network in europe once it's  constructed  but what they did for in terms of  adaptation was  looking at these two aspects right one  looking at very very detailed high level  the best  climate models there is to like to  basically  uh locate the um systems where they're  most vulnerable  and then the second thing that they did  was use this technique called what-if  scenarios  so basically they laid out all of the  worst case scenarios to  or the case scenario in terms of  flooding in terms of you know different  natural disasters  and how they're going to interact with  climate change and basically come up  with a set of procedures  as to okay scenario a is going to happen  this is a set of procedures that you  have to follow  scenario b you know we can never predict  the future  but it does mean that if we take this  kind of adaptive  approach it means that we have a sense  of how the future might unfold  and we are prepared for it and michael  mann very briefly  do you feel optimistic the real change  is 